rics by cell line similarity group.\n- Metrics shown are MAE and Pearson Δ.\n- No model architecture or interface is visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 56, "caption": "Supplementary Figure 7 Architecture ablations, training efficiency, and scaling analysis for X-Cell-Ultra. (A) Architecture and loss ablation on the 4.9B model (Replogle-Nadig, 6 epochs). Scatter shows δ -norm ratio ˆ ρ vs. DE Pearson r for six configurations; color = MAE. The red shaded region (ˆ ρ < 0 . 2) indicates conservative collapse (predicting small effects). Replacing scale-invariant losses with CCC and removing the hidden residual shortcut progressively recovered both magnitude (ˆ ρ : 0 . 22 → 0 . 51) and direction (DEr : 0 . 05 → 0 . 33); see Table A1. (B) Model FLOPs Utilization (MFU) comparison. X-Cell-Ultra achieves 41% MFU on 64 H200 GPUs, comparable to Llama 3 (reported 38-43% [95]) and ∼ 20 × the 55M X-Cell (2%). (C) Normalized loss scaling (relative to 83M model). Both train (dashed, α = 0 . 32, R 2 = 0 . 96) and test (solid, α = 0 . 03, R 2 = 0 . 97) follow power laws; color = DE Pearson r . (D) Individual validation metrics vs. trainable parameters: centroid accuracy (left) and per-perturbation MAE (right), colored by validation loss. (E) Training dynamics over 20 epochs: test loss (left) and DE Pearson r (right) for all five model sizes.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A-E, showing machine learning model scaling and evaluation metrics.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing `DE Pearson r` on x-axis against `δ-norm ratio (ρ)` on y-axis. Points correspond to methods or model variants labeled `60M baseline`, `Tied + QK-Norm`, `CCC+dnp (residual)`, `MLP head (no resid)`, `Tied + per-gene CCC (no resid)`, and `add tied`. Colorbar indicates `MAE`.\n\nPanel B: Horizontal bar chart of `Model FLOPs Utilization (%)`. Rows include `scGPT`, `X-CELL`, `Llama 3`, and `X-Cell-Ultra`. Labels include parameter sizes such as `53M`, `55M`, `405B`, and `4.9B`, with utilization values around `1%`, `2%`, `40%`, and `41%`. A dashed vertical reference is labeled `LLM-grade`.\n\nPanel C: Scaling-law style plot with `Trainable Parameters` on the x-axis and `Normalized Loss (relative to 83M)` on the y-axis. Points are labeled `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Blue dashed line represents train behavior and pink line represents test behavior. Colorbar indicates `DE Pearson r`.\n\nPanel D: Two side-by-side scaling plots. Left plot shows `Control Accuracy` versus `Trainable Parameters`, increasing from `83M` through `3.1B`. Right plot shows `Per-gene MAE` versus `Trainable Parameters`, decreasing as model size increases. Colorbar indicates `Val Loss`.\n\nPanel E: Two training curves over `Epoch`. Left plot shows `Test Loss` decreasing over epochs for model sizes `83M`, `216M`, `543M`, `1.6B`, and `3.1B`. Right plot shows `DE Pearson r` increasing over epochs for the same model sizes.\n\nBiological source objects: none visibly depicted. The figure contains quantitative plots related to model performance, trainable parameters, loss, MAE, control accuracy, and differential expression Pearson correlation.\n\nMain visible finding: larger trainable parameter counts generally correspond to lower loss/MAE and higher control accuracy or DE Pearson correlation, with model performance improving across epochs and scaling comparisons shown across model sizes."]}]

